Classify a file in the in-world filesystem.

usage: @ftype <path>

Reports the kind of the file at an absolute VFS path: "text", "binary", or
"ELF". A directory reports "directory". This is a dumbed-down file(1): it only
tells these apart, so you can see whether @fcat will show a file or whether it
is a compiled image to download with @fget.

A file is ELF when it begins with the ELF magic bytes. It is binary when it
holds a NUL byte or too many non-text bytes near its start; otherwise it is
text.

Wizard only.

Example:
  @ftype /bin/greet.elf
      Reports ELF.

See also @fls, @fcat, @fget.
